//./io_benchmark --size 500
I/O Benchmark
=============
Test file size: 500 MB
Note: For clean results, run manually before:
  sync && sudo sh -c 'echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches'
========================================
Benchmark: I/O Methods Comparison
========================================
File size: 500 MB
Buffer size: 64 KB (for fwrite/write)
=== fwrite() with buffer=65536 bytes ===
Time: 0.622 seconds
Throughput: 804.11 MB/s
=== write() with buffer=65536 bytes ===
Time: 0.785 seconds
Throughput: 636.58 MB/s
=== write() with O_SYNC (buffer=65536 bytes) ===
Time: 18.800 seconds
Throughput: 26.60 MB/s
Note: O_SYNC forces physical write to disk on each write() call
=== mmap() ===
Time: 1.741 seconds
Throughput: 287.22 MB/s
=== write() with O_DIRECT (buffer=65536 bytes) ===
Time: 1.308 seconds
Throughput: 382.38 MB/s
Note: O_DIRECT bypasses page cache (direct to disk)
      Requires aligned memory and buffer size
=== Summary ===
Fastest method: (compare results above)
Factors affecting performance:
- stdio (fwrite) has user-space buffering
- write() goes directly to kernel, but still uses page cache
- mmap() allows direct memory access, lazy writes
- O_SYNC forces physical write to disk on each call (VERY slow)
- O_DIRECT bypasses page cache entirely (direct to disk)
- Actual disk speed depends on: HDD vs SSD, filesystem, etc.
